<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Cape Verde</h1><p class="st-meta">1462 CE – 1975 CE · capital Praia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Atlantic island colony that became a key hub of the early slave trade and birthplace of a Creole society, until independence in 1975.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The uninhabited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> islands were reached by navigators in Portuguese service between 1456 and 1462. Settlement began on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> under the Genoese captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-noli">Antonio de Noli</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cidade-velha">Ribeira Grande</a>, today's Cidade Velha, became one of the first European towns in the tropics.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cidade-velha">Ribeira Grande</a> served as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepot</a> for enslaved Africans shipped to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and the islands grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and bred horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Portuguese settlers and enslaved Africans formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> society with its own language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verdean-creole">Kriolu</a>, and music. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia</a> in 1770. Repeated droughts caused famines that killed tens of thousands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 20th century Cape Verdeans joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral">Amilcar Cabral's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a>, which fought for the independence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a> and the islands. After the 1974 revolution in Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> became independent on 5 July 1975.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-cape-verde.jpg" alt="Portuguese Cape Verde" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1462 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Praia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antonio de Noli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese and Cape Verdean Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cape Verde</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cape Verde (colony), Colony of Cape Verde</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=portuguese-cape-verde" aria-label="Portuguese Cape Verde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4038 1623 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Cape Verde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4358 1879L4773 1044M4358 1879L4572 1991"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M4572 1991h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4358 1879h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4358 1879h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Praia. <a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=portuguese-cape-verde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

